## Final learning progression

We will follow this exact sequence:

### Topic 1 — Tool calling fundamentals

Learn:

* What tools are
* Why LLMs need tools
* Tool schemas
* Tool descriptions
* Tool selection
* Tool arguments
* Tool results
* Tool calls vs normal text responses
* The basic agent loop
* What the LLM does vs what the application does

### Topic 2 — Build a basic agent

Use the existing OpenRouter setup and implement a simple agent with:

```text
add_numbers(a, b)
```

Learn how the model decides to call the tool and how our Python application executes it.

### Topic 3 — MCP fundamentals

Learn:

* What MCP is
* Why MCP exists
* MCP client
* MCP server
* MCP tools
* MCP resources
* Tool discovery
* Tool invocation
* MCP messages / protocol concepts
* Agent vs MCP responsibilities

Do not jump into implementation until the concepts are clear.

### Topic 4 — Build our first MCP server

Build a small MCP server exposing:

```text
add_numbers
```

Keep it intentionally simple.

Learn:

* MCP server initialization
* Tool definition
* Input schema
* Tool execution
* Tool result
* Running the MCP server
* Testing the MCP server independently

### Topic 5 — Bind MCP tools to the agent

Connect the agent to the MCP server.

Target architecture:

```text
User
 ↓
Agent
 ↓
MCP Client
 ↓
MCP Server
 ↓
add_numbers
 ↓
Result
 ↓
Agent
 ↓
Final answer
```

Learn:

* MCP client
* MCP tool discovery
* Loading MCP tools into the agent
* Tool invocation
* Returning results to the model
* Difference between local Python tools and MCP tools

### Topic 6 — MCP + PostgreSQL

Add:

```text
get_user_by_id(user_id)
```

The MCP tool should use the existing PostgreSQL database.

Learn:

* MCP → service layer → database
* Structured tool inputs
* Structured results
* Database errors
* Resource ownership
* Why the LLM should never receive unrestricted database access

### Topic 7 — MCP security

Learn:

* Authentication context
* Authorization
* RBAC
* Permission checks
* Input validation
* Tool boundaries
* Trusted vs untrusted data
* Prompt injection
* Tool poisoning concepts
* Why the LLM must never control permissions
* Why tool arguments must be validated
* Why MCP is not itself an authorization system
* Read vs write tools
* Audit logging
* Rate limits and execution limits

Apply these concepts to the mini-project.

### Topic 8 — Agent + RAG

Reuse my existing RAG knowledge.

Introduce:

```text
Agent
 ↓
RAG retrieval
 ↓
Qdrant
 ↓
Context
 ↓
Agent response
```

Then combine:

```text
Agent
 ├── RAG
 ├── MCP tools
 └── normal conversation
```

Learn when the agent should:

* Answer directly
* Retrieve knowledge
* Call an MCP tool
* Use multiple tools
* Refuse or escalate

### Topic 9 — LangGraph

Use LangGraph to make the agent workflow explicit.

Learn:

* State
* Nodes
* Edges
* Conditional routing
* Tool nodes
* State transitions
* Retry handling
* Error handling
* Checkpoints / persistence where relevant
* Human approval
* Bounded execution

Compare:

```text
Simple agent loop
```

with:

```text
Explicit LangGraph workflow
```

Explain why and when LangGraph is useful instead of simply using an agent loop.

### Topic 10 — Multi-agent

Only introduce multi-agent architecture if there is a genuine reason.

Start from the working single-agent implementation.

Potential architecture:

```text
                 Supervisor
                     │
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        ↓            ↓            ↓
 Knowledge Agent  User Agent   Task Agent
        │            │            │
        └────────────┼────────────┘
                     ↓
                  MCP Tools
```

Learn:

* Why multiple agents may be useful
* When multiple agents are unnecessary
* Supervisor pattern
* Specialist agents
* Agent handoffs
* Shared state
* Communication
* Tool ownership
* Error propagation
* Security boundaries
* Evaluation of multi-agent workflows

Do not create multiple agents merely for the sake of using multiple agents.

